WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:04.660
The map has islands.

2
00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:07.280
Why do they become helices and sheets?

3
00:00:08.333 --> 00:00:11.373
Each island is the geometry of a regular shape.

4
00:00:12.173 --> 00:00:14.813
That shape lets the backbone hydrogen bond to itself.

5
00:00:15.867 --> 00:00:19.607
The bonds run between backbone C=O groups and N–H groups.

6
00:00:20.567 --> 00:00:22.887
Shapes built this way are called secondary structure.

7
00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:25.830
Secondary structure is local.

8
00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:29.330
It describes a stretch of chain, not the whole protein.

9
00:00:30.383 --> 00:00:32.023
First, the alpha helix.

10
00:00:32.803 --> 00:00:34.003
It is a right-handed coil.

11
00:00:35.033 --> 00:00:38.053
It has a little, over three and a half residues per turn.

12
00:00:39.033 --> 00:00:43.973
Inside the helix, each C=O group bonds to the N–H group four residues ahead.

13
00:00:44.967 --> 00:00:49.127
In the first turn, the N–H groups have no partner four residues back.

14
00:00:49.987 --> 00:00:51.227
The side chains stick outward.

15
00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:55.420
In a helix, the partners are near each other in the sequence.

16
00:00:56.483 --> 00:00:57.883
Second, the beta sheet.

17
00:00:58.683 --> 00:01:00.323
Its strands are stretched nearly flat.

18
00:01:01.283 --> 00:01:04.143
Side chains alternate, above and below the sheet.

19
00:01:05.117 --> 00:01:07.537
Each strand bonds to its neighbouring strands.

20
00:01:08.397 --> 00:01:11.497
They can run in the same direction, or in opposite directions.

21
00:01:12.567 --> 00:01:15.167
Those strands can lie far apart in the sequence.

22
00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.040
Now meet a rule-breaker, proline.

23
00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:22.280
Its side chain loops back and bonds to its own backbone nitrogen.

24
00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:24.540
That makes a ring.

25
00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:29.080
So proline has no N–H group to donate, and its ring is bulky.

26
00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:34.750
Here is the test. Where in a helix could a proline sit without breaking it?

27
00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:42.170
In the first turn.

28
00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:45.410
There, the N–H groups have no partners anyway.

29
00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:47.550
Losing one costs nothing.

30
00:01:48.583 --> 00:01:50.763
In the middle, proline breaks the helix.

31
00:01:51.583 --> 00:01:53.263
It cannot donate its hydrogen bond.

32
00:01:54.283 --> 00:01:57.463
And its ring bumps into the backbone of the turn before it.

33
00:01:58.383 --> 00:02:00.483
Its fixed angle is not the problem.

34
00:02:01.383 --> 00:02:02.963
That angle is a helical one.

35
00:02:03.883 --> 00:02:06.023
The same ring builds something else, collagen.

36
00:02:06.983 --> 00:02:09.503
It pre-shapes each strand into an extended helix.

37
00:02:10.433 --> 00:02:13.703
Collagen repeats a pattern: Gly-X-Y.

38
00:02:13.703 --> 00:02:17.393
X and Y are often proline and hydroxyproline.

39
00:02:18.367 --> 00:02:21.187
Three chains wind together into a triple helix.

40
00:02:21.947 --> 00:02:24.387
Every third residue sits at the crowded center.

41
00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:27.810
Only glycine is small enough to fit there.

42
00:02:28.733 --> 00:02:32.813
Put a larger residue in place of that glycine, in type I collagen.

43
00:02:33.653 --> 00:02:35.953
That is a common cause of osteogenesis imperfecta, brittle-bone disease.

44
00:02:37.033 --> 00:02:39.133
What kills one structure builds another.

45
00:02:40.013 --> 00:02:42.053
One residue, again, decides the shape.

46
00:02:43.033 --> 00:02:45.333
Geometry allows these local shapes.

47
00:02:45.993 --> 00:02:48.413
But it does not choose which ones a protein makes.

48
00:02:49.417 --> 00:02:52.557
Helices and sheets are the backbone bonding to itself.
